<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    THE EIGHTH DAY - biomes.xml
    ===========================
    Where the four deposits actually appear in the world.

    THIS IS THE LEAST CERTAIN FILE IN THE MOD, AND IT IS BUILT TO BE
    ==============================================================
    Every other patch here targets a vanilla element whose shape is well
    established and widely modded. World generation is not that: decoration
    placement has moved between biomes.xml and rwgmixer.xml across versions, and
    the element name below cannot be confirmed without the game.

    So the design does not lean on it. Every recipe the deposits feed keeps the
    route it already had, and the deposit route is added ALONGSIDE. If this file
    matches nothing:

      * the patches no-op silently, as XPath patches do
      * no deposit ever spawns
      * the four ores become unobtainable
      * and nothing else changes, because nothing requires them

    The cost of being wrong here is an efficiency and an errand. That is the whole
    reason the recipes were written as alternatives rather than replacements.

    HOW TO FIX IT IN ONE LINE (VERIFY-25)
    Run ./tools/check-vanilla.py against a real Data/Config. It reports each
    xpath that matches nothing, by name. Then either:

      * the container element is named differently - change the four xpaths below
      * decorations moved out of biomes.xml entirely - move these four appends
        into a new Config/rwgmixer.xml with the target that file uses

    Either way it is four lines in one file, which is why they all live here and
    nowhere else.

    THE PROBABILITIES
    Deliberately low. A deposit the player trips over is scenery; one they have to
    look for is a destination. Limestone is roughly three times commoner than the
    rest because flux is a tier-1 need and the whole metal chain waits on it.

    Scheelite is in the wasteland and nowhere else, at the lowest probability in
    the file. That is the point of it: the top of the metal chain is somewhere you
    have to decide to go.
-->
<configs>

    <!-- PINE FOREST and PLAINS - limestone only.

         This does not break the "starter biomes stay clean" rule in
         entitygroups.xml. That rule is about what can kill you. A limestone
         outcrop is a reason to walk up the hill, not a reason to die on it. -->

    <append xpath="/biomes/biome[@name='pine_forest']/decorations">
        <decoration type="block" blockname="edOreLimestone" prob="0.0012" />
    </append>

    <append xpath="/biomes/biome[@name='plains']/decorations">
        <decoration type="block" blockname="edOreLimestone" prob="0.0012" />
    </append>


    <!-- DESERT - bitumen, and limestone in the exposed rock.
         The desert already teaches Carrion Hounds; now it also pays. -->

    <append xpath="/biomes/biome[@name='desert']/decorations">
        <decoration type="block" blockname="edOreLimestone" prob="0.0014" />
        <decoration type="block" blockname="edOreBitumen" prob="0.0008" />
    </append>


    <!-- BURNT FOREST - bitumen and sulphur. Both fit the ground: this is where
         things burned, and it is where the chemistry inputs are. -->

    <append xpath="/biomes/biome[@name='burnt_forest']/decorations">
        <decoration type="block" blockname="edOreBitumen" prob="0.0007" />
        <decoration type="block" blockname="edOreSulphur" prob="0.0007" />
    </append>


    <!-- WASTELAND - sulphur, and the only scheelite in the world.

         By the time a player wants carbide they have met what the wasteland
         holds. Putting the top of the metal chain here is the mod being
         consistent with itself: the difficulty selector is the map. -->

    <append xpath="/biomes/biome[@name='wasteland']/decorations">
        <decoration type="block" blockname="edOreSulphur" prob="0.0010" />
        <decoration type="block" blockname="edOreScheelite" prob="0.0005" />
    </append>

</configs>
